--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -18,4 +18,23 @@ The empty pointer returns the document itself. Tokens decode `~1` as `/` and `~0
 Invalid syntax, missing values, and invalid or out-of-range array indices throw an error.
 URI fragment strings such as `#/users/0` are not accepted. The document is not modified.
 
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies an array of JSON Patch operations as defined by
+[RFC 6902](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902.html):
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+applyPatch({ users: ["Ada"] }, [
+  { op: "add", path: "/users/-", value: "Grace" },
+  { op: "test", path: "/users/0", value: "Ada" },
+]); // { users: ["Ada", "Grace"] }
+```
+
+Supports `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, and `test`, applied in order.
+Returns a deep copy without modifying the document or patch, including when an operation fails.
+Invalid patches, missing required locations, and failed tests throw errors. Object equality for
+`test` ignores member order. Paths and `from` locations use plain JSON Pointers; the empty path
+addresses the entire document. Removing the root returns `undefined`, representing an absent
+document; a subsequent `add` at the empty path restores it.
+
 Run the tests with `npm test` (Node.js 22.18 or newer, with TypeScript support enabled).
